; expect 42
; A bare reference inside a module binds to that module's own
; definition, so importing a module cannot change what that module
; does.
;
; It used to. `Shadowed.viaOwn` calling its own `helper` reached the
; ENTRY FILE's `helper` whenever the entry defined one - in a program
; both compilers accepted, neither diagnosed, and both got wrong the
; same way, which is why no differential gate could see it. A
; namespace in which importing a module rewrites that module's calls
; is not a namespace.
;
; Three terms, and each fails differently if resolution is wrong:
;
;   (viaOwn 4)     40 - the module's own `helper`. The old rule gave 5.
;   (helper 4)      5 - the entry file's own reference to its own
;                       definition, which must not have moved.
;   (viaImport 4) 104 - a name the module does NOT define, reached
;                       through a module it imports. This is the term
;                       that fails if the new rule REPLACED the old
;                       lookup instead of preceding it.
(import Shadowed)

(:: helper (-> Int Int))
(fn (helper x) (+ x 1))

(:: main Int)
(fn (main)
  (if (== (+ (+ (viaOwn 4) (helper 4)) (viaImport 4)) 149) 42 1))
